<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#medism">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Medism</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Medism was the Greek term for collaborating with or siding with the Persian Empire, whose people the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> often called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a>. It was a charge of treason during and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-persian-wars">Persian Wars</a> of the fifth century BCE, levelled at Thebes and other states that submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> in 480 BCE and at individuals such as the Spartan regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pausanias-general">Pausanias</a>. The word reflects how closely Greeks linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> with its Median predecessor.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/medism.jpg" alt="Medism" width="320" height="173" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Philippe Chavin (Simorg) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Persepolis_-_Royal_audience_low-relief.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
